virglrenderer: a framebuffer without attachments gets a depth stand-in

What: when the guest's framebuffer has no colour and no depth/stencil buffer and
the host has no ARB_framebuffer_no_attachments, a DEPTH_COMPONENT16
renderbuffer is attached to the host framebuffer while the guest draws
without attachments: detached when the guest binds real buffers
(unless its depth buffer took the place), kept for the next switch and freed
after 64 framebuffer changes without it or with the guest context. It is as
large as the first viewport (grown at draw time, at most 8192x8192 = 128 MiB
and the host's renderbuffer limit), and while it is attached the guest's depth
test is kept off, as GL behaves without a depth buffer; there is no stencil,
so the stencil test passes.
Why: the guest sends such a framebuffer when all its draw buffers are GL_NONE
(WebGL conformance: webgl-draw-buffers.html, conformance2/rendering/
draw-buffers.html). That is legal and writes nothing, but Apple's GL answers the
draw with GL_INVALID_FRAMEBUFFER_OPERATION (missing attachment), and with
check-gl-errors that stopped the whole guest context: Chrome drew nothing for
the rest of its life (WebGL 1 in one Chrome: 430/787 pages). A colour
renderbuffer is no stand-in: Apple calls a framebuffer whose only colour
attachment no draw buffer selects "unsupported". Occlusion queries count every
fragment as without attachments. The guest does not send its framebuffer size
here (only with VIRGL_CAP_FB_NO_ATTACH, which would also give guests the
extension), so the viewport gives the size: with a viewport larger than the
guest's framebuffer a query also counts the fragments outside it.
Upstreamable in spirit (hosts without framebuffer_no_attachments); not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-empty-framebuffer.c (public API on the Mac's OpenGL:
draws without attachments are accepted, the context keeps working, a clear after
switching back covers the whole colour buffer, occlusion queries with the depth
test on count every sample of a 64x48 viewport twice, and of a 32x16 one after
the stand-in was freed and made again; fails without this patch).
